---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import TikiPlaceholder from '../../components/TikiPlaceholder.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import QuoteForm from '../../components/QuoteForm.astro';
import { serviceSchema, breadcrumbSchema, webPageSchema } from '../../lib/schema';

const path = '/tiki-huts/chickee-huts/';
const title = 'What Is a Chickee Hut? Traditional Palm-Thatch Shelters';
const description =
  'What a chickee hut is, how the frame and palm-thatch roof are built, why the steep pitch matters, and how the term differs from tiki hut in South Florida.';

const crumbs = [{ href: '/tiki-huts/', label: 'Tiki Huts' }, { label: 'Chickee Huts' }];

const schema = [
  serviceSchema({
    name: 'Chickee hut construction',
    description,
    path,
    serviceType: 'Chickee hut construction',
    offers: ['Chickee hut construction', 'Traditional palm thatch roofing', 'Chickee re-thatching'],
  }),
  breadcrumbSchema(crumbs, path),
  webPageSchema({ path, name: title, description }),
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} crumbs={crumbs} schema={schema}>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Chickee huts"
    title="Chickee Huts: The Original South Florida Shade Structure"
    lede="Before there were pergolas or patio covers here, there was the chickee — a raised open frame with no walls, carrying a steep roof of layered palm thatch. It is a building form developed by the Seminole and Miccosukee peoples specifically for this heat, this rain and this humidity, and nothing built since has improved on it for pure shade and airflow."
    facts={[
      { label: 'Form', value: 'Open-sided, no walls, steeply pitched thatch roof' },
      { label: 'Frame', value: 'Cypress or pressure-treated timber' },
      { label: 'Roof', value: 'Layered sabal palm thatch, or synthetic where upkeep must be low' },
      { label: 'Same as', value: 'What most people call a tiki hut' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <h2>Chickee, tiki hut, thatched hut — what is the difference?</h2>
      <p>
        For practical purposes, none. <strong>Chickee</strong> is the Seminole and Miccosukee
        word for the open-sided thatched shelter, and it is the older and more accurate term
        for the structure. <strong>Tiki hut</strong> is the name the same form picked up
        commercially in Florida — it borrows from Polynesian imagery rather than from
        anything local, but it is the phrase almost everybody now uses and searches for.
      </p>
      <p>
        You will also see <em>thatched hut</em>, <em>palm hut</em> and{' '}
        <em>tropical thatch roof</em>. They all describe the same thing: a frame, no walls,
        and a thick roof of layered palm fronds at a steep pitch.
      </p>
      <p>
        We use both names on this site because both are correct and people arrive using
        both. What matters is the structure, not the label.
      </p>

      <h2>How a chickee is actually built</h2>
      <p>
        The construction logic is worth understanding, because it explains why the structure
        works and where it goes wrong when it is built badly.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The frame</strong> is timber — traditionally cypress, which is naturally
        durable, and today often pressure-treated stock. Posts are set into the ground or
        onto footings, carrying a ring beam and then the rafters that give the roof its
        pitch. The frame is the permanent part of the structure; done properly it long
        outlasts the roof above it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The roof</strong> is where the craft is. Sabal palm fronds are layered from
        the bottom edge upward, each course overlapping the one below, until the roof is
        thick enough that no water path runs through it. The steep pitch is not a stylistic
        choice — it is what makes water run off the outer surface fast rather than working
        into the layers. A shallow thatch roof leaks; a steep, thickly built one does not.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The open sides</strong> are equally deliberate. Walls would trap heat under a
        roof that is already insulating well. Leaving the structure open lets the air move
        through it, which is why standing under a good chickee on a hot day feels
        dramatically different from standing under a solid roof of the same size.
      </p>

      <h2>What owning one involves</h2>
      <p>
        A thatch roof is a natural, wearing surface. It weathers, thins over time and
        eventually needs renewing — that is the deal, and it is not a defect. How long a
        roof lasts depends on sun exposure, rainfall, whether trees drop debris onto it, and
        above all how thickly and skilfully it was laid.
      </p>
      <p>
        Re-thatching keeps the frame and renews the roof, which is a substantially smaller
        job than building from scratch.{' '}
        <span class="placeholder-token">[THATCH_LIFESPAN]</span> — add the realistic lifespan
        and re-thatch interval here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Synthetic thatch is the low-maintenance alternative: much longer service life, far
        less upkeep, at the cost of some of the texture that makes the real thing worth
        having. For a commercial property or an owner who does not want a maintenance cycle,
        it is often the sensible answer.
      </p>

      <h2>Permits and local rules</h2>
      <p>
        Requirements differ by jurisdiction and by structure, and some building departments
        treat certain traditional chickee structures differently from other accessory
        buildings. That is a genuine legal question about your specific address, not
        something a web page should answer with confidence.{' '}
        <span class="placeholder-token">[PERMIT_POLICY]</span> — fill in how this is handled.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-sand">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Our work</p>
        <h2>Chickee hut photography</h2>
      </div>
      <TikiPlaceholder caption="Photographs of completed chickee huts are being added here." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell form-band">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Ask about a chickee hut</h2>
        <p>
          Tell us where it would go, roughly how large, and whether you want natural or
          synthetic thatch. If you are not sure, describe how you would use it and we will
          make a recommendation.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are still weighing a hut against a modern structure, the{' '}
          <a href="/pergolas/">pergola pages</a> set out the other side of the trade-off.
        </p>
      </div>
      <QuoteForm service="Tiki Hut" compact />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    title="A structure worth getting right."
    body="Thatch is craft work, and the difference between a roof that lasts and one that does not is entirely in how it was laid. Start with a conversation about your space."
    secondaryLabel="Tiki hut construction"
    secondaryHref="/tiki-huts/construction/"
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .form-band { display: grid; gap: var(--space-l); }
  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .form-band { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr); gap: var(--space-2xl); align-items: start; }
  }
</style>
